[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Lists in Java: ArrayList, LinkedList, Vector, and Stack"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Java, the `List` interface is a part of the Collections Framework and represents an ordered collection of elements. It allows duplicate elements and provides methods to access elements by their index. Several classes implement the `List` interface, each with its own characteristics and performance trade-offs. This tutorial covers `ArrayList`, `LinkedList`, `Vector`, and `Stack`."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. ArrayList"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"`ArrayList` is a resizable array implementation of the `List` interface. It's the most commonly used `List` implementation due to its efficient random access and good overall performance. Internally, `ArrayList` uses an array to store elements. When the array is full, it automatically creates a new, larger array and copies the elements."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"1.1. Key Characteristics of ArrayList"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dynamic size: Automatically resizes as needed."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fast random access: Accessing elements by index is O(1)."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Insertion/Deletion: Insertion or deletion in the middle of the list can be slow (O(n)) because elements need to be shifted."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Not synchronized: Not thread-safe by default."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"1.2. ArrayList Example"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class ArrayListExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Creating an ArrayList
        List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

        // Adding elements
        names.add("Alice");
        names.add("Bob");
        names.add("Charlie");

        // Accessing elements
        System.out.println("First name: " + names.get(0));

        // Iterating through the list
        System.out.println("All names:");
        for (String name : names) {
            System.out.println(name);
        }

        // Removing an element
        names.remove(1);
        System.out.println("Names after removing Bob:");
        for (String name : names) {
            System.out.println(name);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates creating an `ArrayList`, adding elements, accessing elements by index, iterating through the list, and removing an element."},{"order":13,"type":"heading","text":"2. LinkedList"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"`LinkedList` is a doubly-linked list implementation of the `List` and `Deque` interfaces. Each element in a `LinkedList` is stored in a node that contains a reference to the previous and next nodes in the list. This structure makes `LinkedList` efficient for insertion and deletion operations, especially in the middle of the list."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"2.1. Key Characteristics of LinkedList"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dynamic size: Automatically resizes as needed."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Efficient insertion/deletion: Insertion and deletion are O(1) if you have a reference to the node."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Slow random access: Accessing elements by index is O(n) because it requires traversing the list from the beginning or end."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Not synchronized: Not thread-safe by default."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"2.2. LinkedList Example"},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.List;

public class LinkedListExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Creating a LinkedList
        List<String> names = new LinkedList<>();

        // Adding elements
        names.add("Alice");
        names.add("Bob");
        names.add("Charlie");

        // Accessing elements
        System.out.println("First name: " + names.get(0));

        // Iterating through the list
        System.out.println("All names:");
        for (String name : names) {
            System.out.println(name);
        }

        // Removing an element
        names.remove(1);
        System.out.println("Names after removing Bob:");
        for (String name : names) {
            System.out.println(name);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates creating a `LinkedList`, adding elements, accessing elements by index, iterating through the list, and removing an element.  Note that the usage is very similar to `ArrayList`, but the underlying implementation and performance characteristics are different."},{"order":23,"type":"heading","text":"3. Vector"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"`Vector` is similar to `ArrayList`, but it is synchronized, meaning it is thread-safe. This synchronization comes with a performance overhead. `Vector` also grows its array by doubling its size when it runs out of space, while `ArrayList` typically increases its size by 50%."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"3.1. Key Characteristics of Vector"},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dynamic size: Automatically resizes as needed."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fast random access: Accessing elements by index is O(1)."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Insertion/Deletion: Insertion or deletion in the middle of the list can be slow (O(n)) because elements need to be shifted."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Synchronized: Thread-safe."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"3.2. Vector Example"},{"order":31,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.List;
import java.util.Vector;

public class VectorExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Creating a Vector
        List<String> names = new Vector<>();

        // Adding elements
        names.add("Alice");
        names.add("Bob");
        names.add("Charlie");

        // Accessing elements
        System.out.println("First name: " + names.get(0));

        // Iterating through the list
        System.out.println("All names:");
        for (String name : names) {
            System.out.println(name);
        }

        // Removing an element
        names.remove(1);
        System.out.println("Names after removing Bob:");
        for (String name : names) {
            System.out.println(name);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates creating a `Vector`, adding elements, accessing elements by index, iterating through the list, and removing an element. Because `Vector` is synchronized, it can be used safely in multi-threaded environments, but at the cost of performance."},{"order":33,"type":"heading","text":"4. Stack"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"`Stack` is a subclass of `Vector` that implements a last-in-first-out (LIFO) stack data structure. It provides methods like `push` to add elements to the top of the stack, `pop` to remove the top element, and `peek` to view the top element without removing it."},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"4.1. Key Characteristics of Stack"},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"LIFO: Last-In-First-Out data structure."},{"order":37,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Synchronized: Inherits synchronization from `Vector`."},{"order":38,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Uses Vector: Implemented as a subclass of `Vector`."},{"order":39,"type":"subheading","text":"4.2. Stack Example"},{"order":40,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Stack;

public class StackExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Creating a Stack
        Stack<String> names = new Stack<>();

        // Pushing elements onto the stack
        names.push("Alice");
        names.push("Bob");
        names.push("Charlie");

        // Peeking at the top element
        System.out.println("Top element: " + names.peek());

        // Popping elements from the stack
        System.out.println("Popped: " + names.pop());
        System.out.println("Popped: " + names.pop());
        System.out.println("Popped: " + names.pop());

        // Checking if the stack is empty
        System.out.println("Is stack empty? " + names.isEmpty());
    }
}"},{"order":41,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates creating a `Stack`, pushing elements onto the stack, peeking at the top element, popping elements from the stack, and checking if the stack is empty."},{"order":42,"type":"heading","text":"5. Choosing the Right List Implementation"},{"order":43,"type":"paragraph","text":"The choice of which `List` implementation to use depends on the specific requirements of your application:"},{"order":44,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`ArrayList`: Use when you need fast random access and don't perform frequent insertions or deletions in the middle of the list."},{"order":45,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`LinkedList`: Use when you need frequent insertions or deletions, especially in the middle of the list, and random access is not a primary concern."},{"order":46,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`Vector`: Use when you need thread safety and are working in a multi-threaded environment. However, consider using `Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<>())` for better performance in some cases."},{"order":47,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`Stack`: Use when you need a LIFO stack data structure. However, `Deque` implementations like `ArrayDeque` are generally preferred for stack operations due to their better performance and more complete API."},{"order":48,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding the characteristics and performance trade-offs of each `List` implementation is crucial for writing efficient and effective Java code."}]
